Working with version 5.1 on HP unix system.

Concerning the double integral of a function of two variables:
We are trying to integrate a function of two variables, where integration 
limits for the inner integral is a function of one of the variables, e.g.

 integral(0,5)   integral(t/4 ,t/2)  f(x,t) dx   dt 

We have tried the 'double.integral.demo.fn'  
in example 3 in the help pages for the integrate procedure.
With e.g. f(x,t) = x*t, our program would be as follows:

double.integral.fn <-
        function()
        {
        fun <- function(t,integrand)
          {
            unlist(lapply(t, function(t,integrand)
             {
              integrate(f=integrand, lower=t/4,
                         upper=t/2)$integral
             }
           , integrand) )
          }
        return(integrate(f=fun,
              lower =0, upper=5, integrand=function(x,t)
                  {t*x }
                        )$integral )
        }

However, we receive an error message to the extent that something is 
not vectorized. We would appreciate suggestions on how to solve this
problem.

Thank you,
Peter Thyregod

Dear S-Plus users:

    A practical SAS code for conducting bioequivalence analysis (BE) on
    maximum concentration (cmax) for a two-way crossover trial using SAS
    PROC GLM could be:

    Proc glm data=data1;
    class subj seq per trt;
    model cmax=seq subj(seq) per trt/ss1 ss2 ss3 ss4;
    test h=seq e=subj(seq);
    estimate 'trt 1 vs trt 2' trt 1 -1;
    lsmeans trt/s;

    Could anyone on the list please advise me how to perform the same
    analysis using S-plus? Or please provide me the proper reading resource
    to study.  When I use <-glm(cmax~seq+subj%in%seq+per+trt) function, I
    cannot get the same results as using SAS.

    Thanks a million for your help and Happy Holidays!

    Henry Sun
